WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.710
So we talked about the GitHub workflow
how you branch, introduce changes on

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.780
commits, and then merge your branch back
into master but that only works if

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.440
you're working on a project that you
have a write access to. Maybe you're

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.940
working on a project that you don't have
collaborator access to, like if I wanted

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.689
to make changes on Free Code Camp. That's
when you would need the open-source

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:28.019
workflow which includes forking. When you
create a fork on github.com you're

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:32.640
creating a copy of that repository but
under your own account so you have write

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.610
access. So let's say I fork Free Code
Camp's repository, now I have write access

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:43.469
to it, I can make commits directly to
that repository but they can't see them

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:45.000
on Free Code Camp yet -

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.000
that's ok - once I create my own fork then
I make commits on my for kind of make

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.920
the changes that I think you know Free
Code Camp should include and then I can

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.989
open a pull request from my fork
directly to free code camps fork the

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:04.710
process is overall a little bit similar
to the branching process because it

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.510
includes another copy of the code making
commits in a safe place and then

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.500
introducing them with a pull request but
the main difference is the ownership of

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.270
the separate repositories the original
fork would be under Free Code Camp my

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.750
for could be under my name. When we
aren't using the branching process

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.360
everything is still a part of that same
repository. So just to review, this fork

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.770
and pull workflow is really helpful in
open-source. It includes creating a fork

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.090
of the repository, making commits there,
and then opening a pull request against

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:36.810
the original fork.

